<h1>Insertion Sort</h1>
<p>
    Insertion Sort works by looping through the array.
    At each step it checks if the current element is in the correct position
    by seeing whether it is greater than the previous element or not.
    If the current element is smaller than the previous, it will swap the two.
    Then, it will move backwards in the array, 
    checking the current and previous and swapping them if needed.
    It repeats this until the elements do not need to be swapped.
    Then it jumps to its original position in the array and continues looping forwards.
    The algorithm is finished once it reached the end of the array, 
    after swapping the last element if need be.
    <br>
    <br>
    Insertion Sort essentially builds a sorted array on the left side of the full array,
    and inserts each element into this sorted section one at a time. 
    This is where it gets its name.
    <br>
    <br>
    Insertion Sort performs quicker than other algorithms with a <b>n<sup>2</sup></b>
    Time Complexity, like Bubble and Selection Sort, but it is still considered slow.
</p>

