<h2>Binary Insertion Sort</h2>
<p>
    Binary Insertion Sort is a variation of Insertion Sort.
    <br>
    While Insertion Sort finds where to insert the current element linearly, 
    by checking each element one by one,
    Binary Insertion Sort uses binary searching to find the correct position
    to insert the element.
    This reduces the number of comparisons needed to sort the array.
    In addition, since it knows where to insert the element, 
    it can store the value of the current element and shift all of the elements
    in the sorted section to the right instead of swapping the current element with each element one by one.
    This reduces the number reads and writes needed to sort the array.
    <br>
    <br>
    Despite these improvements, Binary Insertion Sort still has an average Time Complexity of <b>n<sup>2</sup></b>.
</p>